direction: down

title: |md
  ## How BPF_PROG unpacks trampoline arguments
| {near: top-center; shape: text}

src: "Source code you write" {
  style.fill: "#dbeafe"
  src_code: |md
    ```c
    SEC("fexit/vfs_read")
    int BPF_PROG(on_exit,
                 struct file *f,
                 char *buf,
                 size_t count,
                 loff_t *pos,
                 ssize_t ret)
    {
        /* use args + ret directly */
    }
    ```
  |
}

ctx: "Trampoline ctx array" {
  style.fill: "#fef3c7"
  body: |md
    **Does:** stores each original function
    argument in a `u64` slot.

    **Why next:** the BPF program ABI is
    `int (*)(u64 *ctx)`, but learners want
    typed C parameters.

    ```
    ctx[0] = struct file *
    ctx[1] = char *buf
    ctx[2] = size_t count
    ctx[3] = loff_t *pos
    ctx[4] = ssize_t ret   // fexit only
    ```
  |
}

expanded: "Generated wrapper" {
  style.fill: "#dcfce7"
  body: |md
    **Does:** `BPF_PROG` creates an outer
    ctx-taking function and an inner typed
    function.

    **State change:** positional slots become
    verifier-visible typed arguments.

    ```c
    return ____on_exit(ctx,
        (void *)ctx[0],
        (void *)ctx[1],
        (void *)ctx[2],
        (void *)ctx[3],
        (void *)ctx[4]);
    ```
  |
}

src -> ctx: "load-time attach target provides signature"
ctx -> expanded: "macro casts slots by position"
